## Code Quality

**Issue 1 — FAIL: Timing oracle in login handler (username enumeration)**

`/home/bigred/Sentinel/internal/handler/auth.go`, line 30:
```go
if !ok || auth.VerifyPassword(req.Password, hash) != nil {
```
Short-circuit evaluation means `VerifyPassword` (bcrypt, ~100ms) is skipped entirely when the username does not exist. An attacker can enumerate valid usernames by measuring whether the response takes ~0ms (unknown user) or ~100ms (known user, wrong password). Fix: always call `VerifyPassword` against a dummy hash when the user is not found, e.g.:
```go
hash, ok := creds[req.Username]
if !ok {
    hash = "$2a$10$invalidhashvaluethatisusedtowastetimeXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX"
}
err := auth.VerifyPassword(req.Password, hash)
if !ok || err != nil {
    http.Error(w, `{"error":"invalid credentials"}`, http.StatusUnauthorized)
    return
}
```

**Issue 2 — FAIL: `http.Error` sends `Content-Type: text/plain`, not `application/json`**

Every `http.Error(w, `{"error":"..."}`, ...)` call across all handler files sends a JSON-shaped body with `Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8`. The standard library's `http.Error` hardcodes `text/plain`. Any client checking `Content-Type` before parsing will break. These should use `writeJSON` instead:
```go
writeJSON(w, http.StatusBadRequest, map[string]string{"error": "bad request"})
```
This affects approximately 20 call sites across `auth.go`, `policy.go`, `mode.go`, `namespace.go`, and `handler.go` (the JWT middleware).

**Issue 3 — WARN: Missing `Secure` flag on the session cookie**

`/home/bigred/Sentinel/internal/handler/auth.go`, lines 41–48: The `sentinel_token` cookie sets `HttpOnly: true` and `SameSite: Strict` but omits `Secure: true`. Without it, the browser will send the cookie over plain HTTP, defeating the HttpOnly protection over unencrypted connections. Add `Secure: true` (this is a backend security control; the cookie should be HTTPS-only in production).

**Issue 4 — MINOR: `go vet` passes, no additional issues found.**

---

**Code Quality verdict: Needs fixes**

Critical: timing leak on login (username enumeration), and `http.Error` setting wrong `Content-Type`. The `Secure` cookie flag is a real security concern but may be acceptable if the deployment is always behind TLS termination."
